## std::array — Array (Ruby's Array) method surface.
##
## Zero-Rust-stdlib migration, Phase 2 (Option C). `class Array[T]`
## below carries BOTH the FFI alias decls (`Array_<m>` →
## `ruxen_vec_<m>`, consumed by `Lowerer::resolve_ffi_alias_callee`)
## AND the TRUE surface return types that typeck delegates to via the
## builtin-receiver bridge (`InferenceEngine::bridge_builtin_method`,
## reached for `Ty::Array(_)` because `builtin_bridge::is_delegated_head`
## now includes the Array head). `method_home_key` maps `Ty::Array(_)` →
## `"Array"` (generic suffix stripped); element substitution into the
## declared `T` is done by `substitute_generics_in_return`.
##
## `Array` is NOT in `register_builtins`'s type-scope map — `let x:
## Array[Int]` resolves through the hardcoded arm in `resolve_type_expr`
## that builds `Ty::Array(Int)` directly. The anchor branch creates a
## `DefId` for FFI bookkeeping without inserting into type-scope, so the
## hardcoded type resolver stays authoritative for the receiver TYPE
## while the class below homes the METHODS.
##
## ## Generic-stripping at the call site
##
## MIR mangles the surface generic in the call: `Vec[Int]_push`,
## `Array[String]_clone`, etc. The class shell registers under the
## generic-free parent name `Array`, so the alias-map key is
## `Array_push` / `Array_clone`. The lookup site in
## `mir/lower/expr/method_call.rs` peels the `[...]` segment from the
## mangled callee and retries with the generic-stripped key.
##
## ## ABI notes
##
## Every migrated method's true surface type is ABI-identical to its C
## symbol's wire width: `RuxenVec*` / `void*` / boxed `Option` cross the
## C ABI as I64, `uint64_t` (len/count/capacity) as I64 (= `USize`), and
## `T`-by-value `remove` is I64 for every element head. `ty_to_cranelift`
## lowers each to the matching width; the parity guard
## `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins that the derived widths equal
## the hand-rolled `runtime_signature` table.
##
## ## Residual — typed by the collections.rs resolver arms that run
## ## AHEAD of the bridge (these decls below stay laundered to `Int`
## ## purely for the FFI alias / codegen call rewrite; the resolver arm
## ## supplies the user-visible type)
##
##   * closure combinators — `map` / `select` / `reject` / `reduce` /
##     `find` / `index` / `each` / `each_with_index` / `all?` / `any?` /
##     `partition` / `sort_by` / `select!`: MIR-inlined (closure_inline);
##     `each` keeps its (vestigial) alias decl, the rest never reach
##     codegen as a call. Return types are closure-inferred.
##   * `zip` / `to_h`: the result element type is computed from the ARG
##     (`zip`) or the receiver's tuple element (`to_h`) — not expressible
##     as a static declared return — so the resolver arm computes it.
##     Their decls stay for the FFI alias (`ruxen_vec_zip` /
##     `ruxen_hash_from_iter` emit real calls).
##   * `sum`: carries the E0700 non-numeric-element typeck check.
##   * `get_mut` / `get_var`: alias `ruxen_vec_get_opt` with a `&var T`
##     return that differs from `get`'s `&T`; a second `.rx` alias for
##     the same C symbol with a different wire shape trips E0722. Kept as
##     a lang_intrinsics alias + collections.rs resolver arm.
class Array[T]
  lib "runtime/vec.c"
    # ── migrated: true surface types, delegated via the bridge ──
    def self.new as "ruxen_vec_new" -> Array[T]
    def self.with_capacity as "ruxen_vec_with_capacity"(cap: Int) -> Array[T]
    def size as "ruxen_vec_len" -> USize
    def capacity as "ruxen_vec_capacity" -> USize
    def count as "ruxen_vec_count" -> USize
    def empty? as "ruxen_vec_is_empty" -> Bool
    def get as "ruxen_vec_get_opt"(i: Int) -> Option[&T]
    def first as "ruxen_vec_first" -> Option[T]
    def last as "ruxen_vec_last" -> Option[T]
    def include? as "ruxen_vec_contains_int"(x: T) -> Bool
    def clone as "ruxen_vec_clone" -> Array[T]
    def to_a as "ruxen_vec_clone" -> Array[T]
    def reverse as "ruxen_vec_reverse" -> Array[T]
    def sort as "ruxen_vec_sort" -> Array[T]
    def join as "ruxen_vec_join"(sep: &String) -> String
    def push as "ruxen_vec_push"(x: T)
    def pop as "ruxen_vec_pop" -> Option[T]
    def clear as "ruxen_vec_clear"
    def truncate as "ruxen_vec_truncate"(n: Int)
    def swap as "ruxen_vec_swap"(i: Int, j: Int)
    def insert as "ruxen_vec_insert"(i: Int, x: T)
    def remove as "ruxen_vec_remove"(i: Int) -> T
    def extend as "ruxen_vec_extend"(other: Array[T])
    def dedup as "ruxen_vec_dedup"
    # Ruby `take(n)` / `drop(n)` eager-materialise a fresh Array.
    def take as "ruxen_vec_take"(n: Int) -> Array[T]
    def drop as "ruxen_vec_skip"(n: Int) -> Array[T]
    # `a.chain(b)` concatenates two arrays directly.
    def chain as "ruxen_vec_chain"(other: Array[T]) -> Array[T]
    # `arr.to_set` → Set[T].
    def to_set as "ruxen_set_from_iter" -> Set[T]
    # ── residual: laundered `Int` decls kept ONLY for the FFI alias;
    #    the collections.rs resolver (ahead of the bridge) supplies the
    #    user-visible return type. See header.
    def sum as "ruxen_vec_sum" -> Int
    def each as "ruxen_vec_each" -> Int
    def zip as "ruxen_vec_zip"(other: Int) -> Int
    def to_h as "ruxen_hash_from_iter" -> Int
  end
end
